.TH OKAGENT 1 "2026-09-29" "okagent 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
okagent \- SSH agent backed by an OnlyKey hardware token
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B status
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B pubkey
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B completions
.I shell
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B run
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR identity .\|.\|.]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.B \-\-
.I command
.RI [ arg .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B shell
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR identity .\|.\|.]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B ssh
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR identity .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR host ]
.I identity
.RI [ ssh-arg .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B mosh
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR identity .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR host ]
.I identity
.RI [ mosh-arg .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B ssh-copy-id
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR host ]
.I identity
.RI [ ssh-copy-id-arg .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B serve
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-daemon ]
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR identity .\|.\|.]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B okagent
is an SSH agent whose private keys live on an
.UR https://onlykey.io
OnlyKey
.UE
hardware token.
No key material is written to disk: for every token-backed operation the token
re-derives the key from an
.I identity
string such as
.BR ferris@example.com ,
and every signature must be confirmed on the device by entering a
three-digit challenge on its buttons.
A plain ed25519, RSA, ECDSA or DSA private key can instead be loaded into a
running agent with
.B ssh-add
(see below), in which case it is held in the agent's memory and signed in
software, with no token involvement.
.PP
Given the same identity and curve,
.B okagent
derives the same public key as the Python
.B onlykey\-agent
program, so existing
.I authorized_keys
entries keep working.
.PP
Keys that the OnlyKey app has written into one of the token's sixteen ECC
slots or four RSA slots ("stored keys") can be used as well, by naming the
slot with
.B \-\-slot
or the
.B slot
configuration key.
The identity then only labels the key; it is not involved in signing.
RSA keys exist only as stored keys.
.PP
An identity has the form
.RI [ user @] host .
A
.IB scheme ://
prefix, a
.BI : port
suffix and a
.BI / path
suffix are accepted and ignored.
Non-ASCII characters are transliterated to ASCII before the identity is
hashed; a warning is logged when this happens.
Identities may be omitted from the command line when the configuration file
lists them (see
.BR "CONFIGURATION FILE" ),
or when a public key file names them (see
.BR \-\-pubkey\-file ).
.PP
The agent implements the
.IR "request identities" ,
.IR "sign request" ,
.I add
and
.I remove
operations of the SSH agent protocol.
.BR "ssh-add \-s " provider
adds one key to a running agent, and
.BR "ssh-add \-e " provider
removes it again, without restarting the agent.
The
.I provider
is either a bare
.RI [ user @] host
for a derived ed25519 key, or a full key label as printed by
.BR "pubkey" :
.BI <ssh:// identity | curve [| slot ]>.
The key is read from the OnlyKey when it is added, so an absent or locked
device, an empty slot or an unparsable
.I provider
is reported as
.BR SSH_AGENT_FAILURE .
If the key's public key is already known, though, the device is not touched:
start the agent with
.B \-\-pubkey\-file
and adding one of those identities is served from the file, so
.B ssh-add \-s
succeeds even while the OnlyKey is unplugged.
Answer the PKCS#11 PIN prompt with an empty passphrase.
The lifetime constraint of
.B ssh-add \-s \-t
is honoured and the PIN is ignored. The confirm constraint
.RB ( "ssh-add \-c" )
is refused, since the agent cannot ask before each signature, as are
destination or certificate constraints.
A private key file given directly to
.B ssh-add
.RB ( SSH2_AGENTC_ADD_IDENTITY )
is loaded into the agent: the key is held in memory and every signature is
made in software, with no OnlyKey and no challenge, even while another key is
being served. ed25519, RSA, ECDSA
.RB ( ecdsa-sha2-nistp256 ,
.IR \-nistp384 " and " \-nistp521 )
and DSA
.RB ( ssh-dss )
keys are accepted, and the key is forgotten when the agent exits.
An RSA key under 1024 bits is refused, as OpenSSH refuses it. DSA only
works with OpenSSH builds that still enable
.IR ssh-dss .
A FIDO security key
.RB ( sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com
or
.IR sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com )
is also accepted: it holds no secret, so every signature is made by the
attached authenticator, which must be touched. See
.BR \-\-fido\-device .
Keys enrolled with
.B ssh-keygen \-O verify-required
need a PIN, which is not supported yet and is refused.
.BR "ssh-add \-d " file
removes only that key again (given the public key of a token-backed identity,
it removes that identity) and
.B ssh-add \-D
removes every identity, token-backed or in-memory, including those from the
configuration, until the agent restarts or they are added back with
.BR "ssh-add \-s" .
The lifetime constraint of
.B ssh-add \-t
is honoured; the confirm constraint and destination or certificate
constraints are refused, as with
.BR "ssh-add \-s" .
Anything else not listed here is refused.
.B ssh-add \-x
locks the agent with a passphrase: until
.B ssh-add \-X
unlocks it with the same passphrase, it lists no keys and refuses to sign,
add or remove keys.
This lock is the SSH agent's own and is separate from the OnlyKey's PIN.
The CLI registers no protocol extensions, so extension requests are answered
with the extension-failure code and the SSH protocol 1 key listing with an
empty list, as
.BR ssh-agent (1)
itself does.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print the firmware version and lock state of the attached OnlyKey.
.TP
.BI pubkey " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.]
Print the public key of each identity, one per line, in
.I authorized_keys
format.
The comment field is
.BI <ssh:// identity | curve >
where
.I curve
is
.B ed25519
or
.BR nist256p1 ,
matching the Python agent.
A stored key's comment has the slot as a third field,
.BI <ssh:// identity | curve | ECC n >
or
.BI <ssh:// identity |rsa|RSA n >,
so that a derived and a stored key for the same identity can be told apart.
.TP
.BI completions " " shell
Print a completion script for
.I shell
(one of
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR powershell )
on standard output, for example
.BI "okagent completions bash > " /etc/bash_completion.d/okagent .
The packaged builds install scripts for bash, zsh and fish already.
.TP
.BI run " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.] " \-\- " command " " [ arg \fR.\|.\|.]
Start a temporary agent on a private socket, run
.I command
with
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
pointing at it and
.B SSH_AGENT_PID
naming the
.B okagent
process, and stop the agent when the command exits.
.B okagent
exits with the command's exit status.
.TP
.BI shell " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.]
Like
.BR run ,
but runs
.B $SHELL
(or
.I /bin/sh
if unset).
.TP
.BI ssh " identity " [ ssh-arg \fR.\|.\|.]
Derive the identity's key and run
.BR ssh (1)
against it.
The host part of the identity is the destination, the user part, if any,
becomes the login name, and a
.BI : port
suffix is passed as
.BR \-p .
The port never affects the key.
.B \-\-host
names a different destination while the identity still names the key; see
.BR \-\-host
below.
.B ssh
is told to use only this key
.RB ( "IdentitiesOnly=yes" )
by way of a temporary public key file.
With
.BR \-i ,
the temporary agent also serves the named identities, so they can be used
through an
.BR \-A
(agent forwarding) connection while the login still signs with the primary
identity's key.
Any further arguments are passed to
.B ssh
after the destination, so they normally form a remote command.
.TP
.BI mosh " identity " [ mosh-arg \fR.\|.\|.]
Like
.BR ssh ,
but runs
.BR mosh (1),
handing it the same
.B ssh
command line through
.BR \-\-ssh .
The destination is
.RI [ user @] host
and any further arguments follow it.
.B \-\-host
overrides the destination.
.B \-i
serves extra identities from the temporary agent, as for
.BR ssh .
.TP
.BI ssh-copy-id " identity " [ ssh-copy-id-arg \fR.\|.\|.]
Install the identity's public key in the remote
.I authorized_keys
by running
.BR ssh-copy-id (1)
against a temporary agent that serves only that key, so both its trial login
and the install sign with the token.
The host part of the identity is the destination and a
.BI : port
suffix is passed as
.BR \-p .
.B \-\-host
overrides the destination.
Any further arguments are passed to
.B ssh-copy-id
before the destination.
The public key comes from
.BR "ssh-add \-L" ,
so no key file is needed and
.B \-i
must not be given.
.TP
.BI serve " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.]
Run a long-lived agent on a unix socket.
Without
.BR \-\-daemon ,
the agent stays in the foreground and prints lines of the form
.PP
.RS
.RS
.EX
SSH_AUTH_SOCK=\fIpath\fR; export SSH_AUTH_SOCK;
SSH_AGENT_PID=\fIpid\fR; export SSH_AGENT_PID;
.EE
.RE
.PP
on standard error, as
.BR ssh-agent (1)
does.
The agent stops on SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP and removes its socket, so
.B ssh-agent \-k
stops it once those variables are exported.
An existing live socket at the path is never replaced.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
The following options apply to every command and may be given before or
after the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read
.I file
instead of the default configuration file.
A missing explicit file is an error; a missing default file is not.
.TP
.BI \-\-curve " curve"
Curve for identities given on the command line, and the default for
configuration entries without one.
.I curve
is
.B ed25519
(the default) or
.B nistp256
(also spelled
.B p256
or
.BR nist256p1 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-slot " slot"
Use the key stored in
.I slot
for the identities given on the command line, instead of deriving one.
.I slot
is
.B ECC1
to
.B ECC16
(a bare number means an ECC slot) or
.B RSA1
to
.BR RSA4 ,
as numbered in the OnlyKey app.
For an ECC slot the
.B \-\-curve
(or its default) must match the key type the slot was written with;
otherwise the command fails and names the curve the slot actually holds.
An RSA slot takes no curve, and giving
.B \-\-curve
with one is an error; whether the slot holds a 2048- or 4096-bit key is
read from the token.
An empty slot is reported as such by the firmware.
This option applies only to identities on the command line; configuration
entries carry their own
.B slot
key.
.TP
.BI \-\-notify\-command " command"
Run
.I command
whenever a signature needs confirming, with the challenge prompt appended
as its last argument.
.I command
is split on whitespace; the first word is the program.
The prompt is still written to the terminal as well.
See
.BR "CHALLENGE PROMPT" .
.TP
.BI \-\-fido\-device " path"
Use the FIDO security key whose hidraw or HID path contains
.I path
for
.B sk-
keys.
By default the first attached authenticator other than the OnlyKey is used
(with several, a warning names the one chosen); give the OnlyKey's own
FIDO interface here to sign with it instead.
The same value can be set with the
.B fido-device
config key.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log more.
Once for informational messages, twice for debug output, three times for
trace output.
Log output goes to standard error unless
.B \-\-log\-file
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " file"
Append log output to
.I file
instead of standard error.
The file is created readable by the owner only.
Useful for a background agent, whose standard error is usually lost.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
The
.BR pubkey ", " run ", " shell ", " serve ", " ssh ", " mosh " and " ssh-copy-id
commands also accept:
.TP
.BI \-\-pubkey\-file " file"
A file of lines as printed by
.BR "okagent pubkey" .
Keys whose comment matches one of the agent's identities are listed to SSH
clients even while the OnlyKey is unplugged or locked, so that
.B ssh
can choose the right key before the device is unlocked.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
When no identities are given on the command line or in the configuration
file, the file also names them: each comment of the form written by
.B okagent pubkey
becomes one identity, in file order, so
.B okagent serve \-\-pubkey\-file
.I file
needs nothing else.
Keys with any other comment are skipped with a warning.
The
.BR ssh ", " mosh " and " ssh-copy-id
commands take a required identity, so they use the file only to preload keys.
.PP
The
.BR ssh ", " mosh " and " ssh-copy-id
commands also accept:
.TP
.BI \-\-host " host"
Connect to
.I host
instead of the host part of the identity, which then only names the key.
.I host
has the form
.RI [ user @] host [: port ]:
a user overrides the login name and a port is passed as
.BR \-p .
A
.I host
without a user keeps the identity's user, so the key's identity and the
login name stay independent.
A
.I host
without a port uses the default port; the identity's
.RI : port
suffix belongs to the identity's host and is not carried over.
This lets one key identity reach several servers, for example
.BI "okagent ssh " key@example.com " \-\-host " admin@server.example.com .
.PP
The
.BR run ", " shell ", " serve ", " ssh " and " mosh
commands also accept:
.TP
.BI \-i " identity, " \-\-identity " identity"
Name an additional identity; repeatable.
For
.BR run ", " shell " and " serve
the identity is appended to the list that would otherwise be used (the
positional identities, else the configuration file's entries, else the
comments of the
.B \-\-pubkey\-file
keys) instead of replacing it.
For
.B ssh
and
.BR mosh ,
which require one primary identity, the extra identities are served by the
temporary agent, so they are available to a forwarded
.RB ( \-A )
connection; the login itself still signs with the primary identity's key.
Like a positional identity, an
.B \-i
identity takes its curve from
.B \-\-curve
and its slot from
.BR \-\-slot .
Give it before the identity, since a literal
.B \-i
meant for ssh must be passed after
.BR \-\- .
.BR pubkey " and " ssh-copy-id
do not accept this option.
.PP
The
.B serve
command also accepts:
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Listen on
.I path
instead of the default socket.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Fork into the background.
The parent prints the
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
and
.B SSH_AGENT_PID
shell snippet on standard output, suitable for
.BR eval ,
and exits.
The child detaches from the terminal and redirects standard input and
output to
.IR /dev/null ;
log output still goes to standard error.
.SH CHALLENGE PROMPT
When an SSH client asks for a signature,
.B okagent
writes a line such as
.PP
.RS
.EX
OnlyKey: enter 3 1 5 to sign as ferris@example.com (ssh-connection login as "ferris"), or press any button if challenge mode is off
.EE
.RE
.PP
to the controlling terminal
.RI ( /dev/tty ),
or to standard error when there is none.
For a stored key the prompt says
.BI "with stored key ECC" n
or
.BI "with stored key RSA" n
after the identity.
The signature completes once the digits are entered on the token.
If the device does not answer within about 22 seconds, is unplugged, is
locked, or the wrong digits are entered, the request fails and the SSH
client is told so; the agent keeps running.
.PP
A background agent has no useful terminal, so use
.B \-\-notify\-command
or the
.B notify\-command
configuration key to hand the prompt to a program, for example
.BR "notify\-send OnlyKey" .
The program receives the whole prompt as its last argument and the parts
separately in the environment variables
.B OKAGENT_DIGITS
(the three digits separated by spaces),
.B OKAGENT_IDENTITY
and, for a stored key,
.B OKAGENT_SLOT
(for example
.BR ECC3 ).
.PP
When a FIDO security key
.RB ( sk-
key) is waiting to be touched, the same terminal and notify command are told
.IR "Security key: touch it to sign as " comment ;
the notify command then sees
.B OKAGENT_TOUCH=1
and
.B OKAGENT_IDENTITY
set to the key's comment, and no digits.
The agent gives up after 30 seconds without a touch.
.PP
Every signature is verified against the public key before it is returned to
the client.
.PP
An RSA key signs a SHA-256 or SHA-512 digest, as chosen by the client's
.B rsa\-sha2\-256
or
.B rsa\-sha2\-512
request; a client asking for the legacy SHA-1
.B ssh\-rsa
signature is refused.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.B okagent
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/okagent/config.toml
(falling back to
.IR ~/.config/okagent/config.toml )
unless
.B \-\-config
or
.B OKAGENT_CONFIG
names another file.
The file is TOML.
Unknown keys are an error.
Command-line options override the corresponding keys.
.TP
.BI curve " = \(dq" curve \(dq
Default curve; see
.BR \-\-curve .
.TP
.BI notify-command " = \(dq" command \(dq
See
.BR \-\-notify\-command .
.TP
.BI socket " = \(dq" path \(dq
Socket path for
.BR serve ;
see
.BR \-\-socket .
.TP
.BI pubkey-file " = \(dq" file \(dq
See
.BR \-\-pubkey\-file .
.TP
.BI log-file " = \(dq" file \(dq
See
.BR \-\-log\-file .
.TP
.BI fido-device " = \(dq" path \(dq
See
.BR \-\-fido\-device .
.TP
.B [[identity]]
One table per identity, used when no identities are given on the command
line.
When there are none here either, the identities come from
.BR pubkey-file .
Each has a required
.B name
key holding the
.RI [ user @] host
string, an optional
.B curve
key that overrides the default curve for that identity only, and an optional
.B slot
key
.RB ( \(dqECC3\(dq ,
the number
.B 3
or
.BR \(dqRSA1\(dq )
that uses the stored key in that slot instead of a derived one; see
.BR \-\-slot .
An RSA slot entry must not have a
.B curve
key.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.EX
curve = "ed25519"
notify-command = "notify-send OnlyKey"
# socket = "/run/user/1000/okagent/agent.sock"
# pubkey-file = "/home/ferris/.ssh/onlykey.pub"
# log-file = "/home/ferris/.local/state/okagent.log"
# fido-device = "/dev/hidraw5"

[[identity]]
name = "ferris@example.com"

[[identity]]
name = "git@github.com"
curve = "nistp256"

[[identity]]
name = "ferris@legacy.example.com"
slot = "ECC3"

[[identity]]
name = "ferris@old.example.com"
slot = "RSA1"
.EE
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OKAGENT_CONFIG
Configuration file to read; same as
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B OKAGENT_LOG
Log filter in
.B tracing_subscriber
.B EnvFilter
syntax, for example
.B debug
or
.BR onlykey_agent=trace .
When set it replaces the level chosen by
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the default configuration file.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Base directory for sockets.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Base directory for sockets on macOS when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.B SHELL
Shell started by
.BR "okagent shell" .
.TP
.BR SSH_AUTH_SOCK ", " SSH_AGENT_PID
Set for the child of
.BR run ", " shell ", " ssh ", " mosh " and " ssh-copy-id .
Printed by
.B serve
for the user to export.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/okagent/config.toml
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/okagent/agent.sock
Default socket for
.BR serve .
If
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset,
.I $TMPDIR/okagent/agent.sock
is used on macOS and
.IR /tmp/okagent\- uid /agent.sock
elsewhere.
The socket and its directory are created with owner-only permissions.
.TP
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/okagent/run\- pid .sock
Temporary socket used by
.BR run ", " shell ", " ssh ", " mosh " and " ssh-copy-id ;
removed on exit.
.TP
.I /dev/hidraw*
On Linux the token is opened directly through the hidraw interface
(on macOS, through IOKit, with no setup needed).
On Linux, a udev rule is needed to open it without root; see the OnlyKey
documentation for
.IR 49\-onlykey.rules .
"Permission denied" from
.B okagent status
means the rule is missing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B okagent
exits 0 on success and 1 on error.
.B run
and
.B shell
exit with the status of the command they ran, or 128 if it was killed by a
signal;
.B ssh
exits with the status of
.BR ssh (1);
.B ssh-copy-id
exits with the status of
.BR ssh-copy-id (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
Publish a key and log in with it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent pubkey ferris@example.com >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.example.com
okagent ssh-copy-id ferris@example.com
okagent ssh ferris@example.com
okagent run ferris@example.com \-\- ssh example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Sign as one identity but connect to another server:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent ssh ferris@example.com \-\-host admin@server.example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use a P-256 key for GitHub and run a command with it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent \-\-curve nistp256 pubkey git@github.com
okagent \-\-curve nistp256 run git@github.com \-\- git push
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use an ed25519 key that the OnlyKey app stored in slot 3:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent \-\-slot ECC3 pubkey ferris@legacy.example.com
okagent \-\-slot ECC3 ssh ferris@legacy.example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the RSA key the OnlyKey app stored in slot 1:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent \-\-slot RSA1 pubkey ferris@old.example.com
okagent \-\-slot RSA1 ssh ferris@old.example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Load a plain ed25519, RSA or ECDSA private key into a running agent (signed in
memory, no token):
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent serve ferris@example.com &
ssh\-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
ssh\-add ~/.ssh/id_ecdsa
ssh\-add \-d ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a background agent for the current shell session:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(okagent serve \-\-daemon ferris@example.com)"
ssh\-add \-L
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the agent as a systemd user service with desktop notifications:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# ~/.config/systemd/user/okagent.service
[Unit]
Description=OnlyKey SSH agent

[Service]
ExecStart=%h/.cargo/bin/okagent serve \-\-notify\-command "notify\-send OnlyKey"
Restart=on\-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
.EE
.RE
.PP
Then
.B systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now okagent
and
.BR "export SSH_AUTH_SOCK=$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/okagent/agent.sock" .
.PP
On macOS, run it from launchd instead; see the README for a
.I ~/Library/LaunchAgents
property list.
.SH NOTES
Only the original OnlyKey is supported, not the OnlyKey DUO.
The agent covers SSH only; GPG and age keys are out of scope.
.SH SEE ALSO
